            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nThere is nothing returned by `goto_hunk()` in case of \"no further hunks\" because the `goto_hunk()` itself never returns anything. Although it is possible to modify it to return `true` / `false` indicating whether a jump was done or not, there is a caveat: it would still show notification in this case even if user plans to later act on the `false` returned value. I don't think it will lead to a good user experience.\n\n> Sometimes I like to cycle through all changes I have made, without using something like diffview.nvim. What I would like to do in this case is grab all files with changes and then traverse through all hunks. This requires knowing when the last hunk in a file has been reached, though I realise my proposal is effectively a presence check, and is subject to much brittleness. If there is any other way to get this information please let me know!\n\nHere are possible approaches for this use case (from the least to the most appropriate, if I were to do it):\n- Write own wrapper around `goto_hunk()` which saves a cursor position prior calling it and later checks if cursor position has changed. If it changed - there was a hunk to move to, if not - there was not. Act after that accordingly.\n- Use [`get_buf_data()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3a354c754656538ad76d1add93ca21e75b7f3181/doc/mini-diff.txt#L478) to get the array of hunks and check if there is a hunk in the target direction relative to the cursor.\n- Use [`vim.fn.setqflist(MiniDiff.export('qf'))`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3a354c754656538ad76d1add93ca21e75b7f3181/doc/mini-diff.txt#L465) to export hunks into quickfix and later use `:copen` to show it. Navigate/use as regular quickfix.\n- If you use Git, use dedicated Git tools to show currently unstaged hunks in a project. For example, [`:Pick git_hunks`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3a354c754656538ad76d1add93ca21e75b7f3181/doc/mini-extra.txt#L446-L447) (requires set up 'mini.pick' and 'mini.extra'). The reason for this is that 'mini.diff' only \"knows\" about hunks from the *currently opened files*, not the entire project. This might be misleading if you expect the latter. 'mini.diff' is specifically designed to work on a buffer level (show difference between buffer's real and reference text), not whole project.\n\nClosing as not planned (but might be implemented in the future if there is a strong use case for it).",
